# HTTP Methods and Postman Practical Report

Date: 27 November 2026

## 1. Objective

To understand HTTP concepts and perform practical testing of different HTTP methods using Postman.

## 2. Tools Used

- Postman
- JSONPlaceholder API
- HTTPBin API

## 3. GET Method

GET method is used to retrieve data from the server.

Practical 1: Fetch a single post.

URL:
https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1

Method: GET

Result:
- Received post details from the server.
- Status Code: 200 OK
- Response format: JSON

Practical 2: Fetch another post.

URL:
https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/2

Method: GET

Result:
- Successfully received Post ID 2.

Practical 3: Fetch posts using Query Parameters.

URL:
https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts?userId=1

Method: GET

Result:
- Received all posts belonging to userId 1.

Practical 4: Change Query Parameter value.

URL:
https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts?userId=2

Method: GET

Result:
- Received posts belonging to userId 2.

Practical 5: Use multiple Query Parameters.

URL:
https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts?userId=2&id=11

Method: GET

Result:
- Received the post matching userId 2 and post ID 11.

Concepts Learned:
- Path Parameters
- Query Parameters
- Multiple Query Parameters
- JSON Response

## 4. POST Method

POST method is used to send data to the server and create a new resource.

URL:
https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts

Method: POST

Body Type: Raw JSON

Request Body:

{
  "title": "Java Full Stack Developer",
  "body": "I am learning REST API using Postman",
  "userId": 1
}

Result:
- Successfully sent JSON data to the server.
- Server returned the submitted data.
- A generated ID was returned.
- Status Code: 201 Created.

Additional Practical:

Changed the title to:

"My First Rest APi"

Sent the request again and received the updated request data in the response.

## 5. Headers Practical

Opened the Headers tab in Postman and inspected automatically generated request headers.

Important Headers:

Content-Type: application/json

Accept: */*

Created a custom header:

Key: Developer

Value: Sufiyan Nagori

Sent the request with the custom header enabled and disabled.

Result:
- Request was successfully processed.
- Status Code: 201 Created.
- Response remained the same because the testing API did not process the custom header.

Concept Learned:
Headers provide additional information with HTTP requests and responses.

Content-Type tells the server the format of the request body.

## 6. PUT Method

PUT method is used to completely update an existing resource.

URL:
https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1

Method: PUT

Body Type: Raw JSON

Request Body:

{
  "id": 1,
  "title": "Update Java developer",
  "body": "I am learning PUT method in Postman",
  "userId": 1
}

Result:
- Successfully sent the PUT request.
- Server returned the updated object.
- Status Code: 200 OK.

Additional Practical:

Performed a GET request again on the same URL.

URL:
https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1

Result:
- Original data was returned instead of the updated data.

Reason:
JSONPlaceholder is a testing API. It returns simulated update responses but does not permanently save changes in its database.

## 7. PATCH Method

PATCH method is used to partially update an existing resource.

URL:
https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1

Method: PATCH

First Request Body:

{
  "title": "Only Title Updated using PATCH"
}

Result:
- Sent only the title field.
- Received the updated title in the response.
- Other fields remained present in the response.
- Status Code: 200 OK.

Second Request Body:

{
  "body": "My body is updated using PATCH"
}

Result:
- Sent only the body field.
- Received the updated body in the response.

Concept Learned:
PATCH updates selected fields instead of sending the complete object.

## 8. DELETE Method

DELETE method is used to send a request to delete an existing resource.

URL:
https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1

Method: DELETE

Result:
- Successfully sent the DELETE request.
- Status Code: 200 OK.
- Response Body: {}

Note:
JSONPlaceholder simulates the DELETE operation. It does not permanently delete data from a real database.

## 9. HTTP Status Codes Practical

Used HTTPBin API to understand different HTTP status codes.

1. 404 Not Found

URL:
https://httpbin.org/status/404

Meaning:
The requested resource was not found.

2. 401 Unauthorized

URL:
https://httpbin.org/status/401

Meaning:
Authentication credentials are missing or invalid.

3. 403 Forbidden

URL:
https://httpbin.org/status/403

Meaning:
The server understood the request but access is not permitted.

4. 500 Internal Server Error

URL:
https://httpbin.org/status/500

Meaning:
An internal server error occurred while processing the request.

5. 400 Bad Request

URL:
https://httpbin.org/status/400

Meaning:
The request contains invalid or incorrect information.

6. 503 Service Unavailable

URL:
https://httpbin.org/status/503

Meaning:
The server is temporarily unable to handle the request.

7. 201 Created

Meaning:
The request successfully created a resource.

